{"response": "To determine which put option must be written on the stock with the lower price, we need to consider the put-call parity (PCP) relationship. The PCP states that the price of a put option plus the present value of the strike price should equal the price of a call option plus the present value of the stock price. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ P + \\frac{K}{(1 + r)^T} = C + S \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( P \\) is the price of the put option,\n- \\( C \\) is the price of the call option,\n- \\( K", "labels": null, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are asked to compare two options with parameters as given. The risk-free interest rate should be assumed to be 6%. Assume the stocks on which these options are written pay no dividends. <image 1> Which put option must be written on the stock with the lower price?\nOptions: A: A, B: B, C: Not enough information, D: nan", "loss": null}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "To determine which put option must be written on the stock with the lower price, we need to consider the put-call parity (PCP) relationship. The PCP states that the price of a put option plus the present value of the strike price should equal the price of a call option plus the present value of the stock price. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ P + \\frac{K}{(1 + r)^T} = C + S \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( P \\) is the price of the put option,\n- \\( C \\) is the price of the call option,\n- \\( K"}]}
